WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.440
Obviously, it helps to be an animal lover
when you start working at a shelter.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:10.310
It’s not exactly a requirement of the job,
but it definitely makes it more palatable

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:15.530
when you occasionally have to deal with a
critter that’s more nasty than usual.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:19.910
Having an already built-in compassion towards
animals makes it easier to forgive them for

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:21.960
not doing what you want.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.359
From a very young age, Harper always liked
to think of animals as people, they just didn’t

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:30.320
think or communicate the same way as humans.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.510
Even the best-trained pets in the world don’t
really know the actual meaning of certain

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:39.200
words, they’re just taught how to respond
to specific commands in certain ways.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.440
But that doesn’t mean that they couldn’t
also have distinct personalities of their

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.970
own, just like humans do.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:51.850
And then, of course, there were all the wild
animals that had never been trained or domesticated.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:56.930
Harper always felt a little sorry for any
baby bird with its wing broken or any badgers

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.710
or opossum that arrived at the shelter after
getting hit by a car.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:05.700
They didn’t live around humans and had probably
never even experienced being indoors before,

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.200
let alone having people nursing them back
to health.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.659
To empathize, Harper used to imagine how terrified
she would be if she was hurt – if she’d

00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:19.969
broken her leg or arm or worse – only for
something much larger than her to take her

00:01:19.969 --> 00:01:23.259
into a structure she’d never been in before.

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:28.819
And on top of that, having no way of understanding
that this giant was trying to help her heal,

00:01:28.819 --> 00:01:34.450
as well as being separated from her friends,
family, and everyone she knew.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:39.130
The way she saw it, that was what it must’ve
been like for the majority of animals that

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:44.420
were brought into the shelter where she worked,
and she wanted to do her best to help calm

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.780
her little furry or feathered patients.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.679
“You’ve got to fortify your heart in this
job, Harp,” her friends who worked with

00:01:50.679 --> 00:01:52.310
her at the shelter would say.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:53.869
And it was true to an extent.

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:58.909
They didn’t mean she had to become cold
and heartless, that would be an overcorrection.

00:01:58.909 --> 00:02:04.229
But it was their – and Harper’s – responsibility
to administer the appropriate medical care

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:09.800
and provide a temporary sanctuary to these
lost, wounded or abandoned animals.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.650
What Harper had to remember was, that none
of them were her own pets.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.930
Often, a lot of the critters that were brought
into the shelter were already someone else’s

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:22.260
companion and might have just got lost while
out on a walk.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.739
And while some others were given up by their
previous owners who had cruelly shrugged off

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:32.360
the responsibility of caring for them, those
animals were in need of new homes, and couldn’t

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.810
stay cooped up in the shelter forever.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.940
Not everyone that gave up their pet did it
callously though.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:42.260
Sometimes they might have found themselves
falling on hard times and being unable to

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.620
financially look after an animal.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:49.170
Others might have owned a pet that had a behavioral
problem – like being too aggressive – and

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:53.450
even though the person still loved them dearly,
they might have just not been able to make

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:54.450
it work.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:59.340
In times like that, they’d have no choice
but to take their animal to the shelter.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.439
Just outside the side entrance was an area
where people could drop their animals off

00:03:03.439 --> 00:03:08.959
and quickly buzz to the staff inside, just
in case they wanted to avoid a protracted,

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:12.420
emotional farewell to their four-legged or
winged companion.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.410
The buzzer had sounded, just as Harper had
been coming off her lunch break.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:20.420
It was her turn, this shift, to quickly go
and collect any animal that had been left

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:21.489
out there.

00:03:21.489 --> 00:03:25.040
Even though their owners usually left them
in their cage or with their lead tied to a

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.920
nearby street lamp, Harper knew she had to
rush to the side door, just in case somebody

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.540
else tried to rush in and steal the poor,
abandoned pet.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.930
After turning them in the lock, she hooked
her keys back on the belt clip she wore and

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:39.970
pushed the side door open.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:44.890
Harper had been fully expecting to see the
usual, a leashed dog or cat coming over to

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:49.269
investigate her, confused as to where their
former owner had gone.

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:53.189
This time though, there was just a box standing
outside.

00:03:53.189 --> 00:03:57.079
It wasn’t huge, in fact, it was almost entirely
empty…

00:03:57.079 --> 00:03:58.129
almost.

00:03:58.129 --> 00:04:03.609
Picking it up carefully, in case there were
baby birds or a nest of unhatched eggs inside,

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:07.040
Harper opened the cardboard flap to reveal…
a rock.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.969
Sitting in the box was a single, solitary
rock, an oval of smooth granite, with a paper

00:04:12.969 --> 00:04:14.510
note next to it.

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.450
Reaching in, she retrieved and unfolded the
scrap.

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:24.360
‘Please care for my pet rock’ was written
on one side, in a messy, uncoordinated scrawl.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.580
Immediately assuming that this whole thing
was some kid’s idea of a joke, Harper tipped

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:32.319
the rock out onto the ground and threw the
box in the trash.

00:04:32.319 --> 00:04:36.360
She had more important things to focus on;
within the next hour alone, people brought

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:41.370
a concussed parakeet, a bat infected with
rabies, and a squirrel that had survived being

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.300
run over by a moped into the shelter.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.930
Work had been so busy that, by the time her
shift had ended, Harper had barely thought

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.789
about the pet rock prank.

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:54.110
That is, until she was getting ready to leave.

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:58.890
She headed through the side door, mainly so
she could do a final check, in case any animals

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.919
had been left there and someone had forgotten
to hit the buzzer.

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:06.130
There was nothing there, and Harper hooked
her bag over her shoulder, shutting the door

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.539
behind her.

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:11.300
Something the size and weight of a baseball
knocked against her foot, causing her to look

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:15.120
down and see the rock, the same one from earlier.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.370
Only now, it had flipped over, probably after
she had tipped it out of its box.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:24.449
On the side facing up at her, the rock had
a shiny metal zipper, fastened shut.

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:27.450
Curious, Harper picked it up to take a closer
look.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:32.160
It didn’t seem like the zip had just been
glued onto the stone surface, it was embedded

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.750
in it, actually part of the rock itself.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.630
And there was something… sad about it.

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:42.660
Sure, it was just a rock, an inanimate lump
of stone.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:48.330
But for whatever reason, a feeling of pity
crept up on Harper, causing her to slip the

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:51.390
rock into her bag as she headed for the parking
lot.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:54.610
It was starting to get dark by the time she
got home.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:59.039
Heading up to her room and flipping on her
lamp, Harper placed the zipped-up stone under

00:05:59.039 --> 00:06:01.889
its light and took another long look at it.

00:06:01.889 --> 00:06:05.910
Although she’d first thought it was purely
a joke, now she couldn’t tell if it was

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:09.800
some elaborate art piece or a quirky garden
ornament.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:14.270
The thing was solid stone all the way through,
surely the zipper was just for show.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:17.740
There couldn’t possibly be anything beneath
it…

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:22.390
To her surprise, the metal fastener began
to part as Harper dragged on the puller and

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:26.160
slider down from the top stop towards the
bottom one.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:31.800
It was relatively easy, not even some of her
own clothes unzipped as smoothly as this rock.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:36.669
As soon as the slider connected with the bottom
stopper, the teeth of the top and bottom edges

00:06:36.669 --> 00:06:39.509
parted, revealing more teeth below…

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:42.539
except these almost looked like human teeth.

00:06:42.539 --> 00:06:48.120
They opened up to reveal a mouth, a part of
the rock just like the zipper which took the

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:49.720
place of its lips.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:55.300
The moment the mouth opened, it let out a
tiny sound, like it was breathing, which startled

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:56.300
Harper.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:00.280
With a yelp, she dropped the rock from her
hands and it even made a tiny grunt as it

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:01.750
fell to the floor.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:03.949
It was alive.

00:07:03.949 --> 00:07:08.430
Seeing it hit her bedroom carpet, Harper stared
utterly bewildered at the rock.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:13.259
Its toothy mouth almost looked sad, like it
had curved into a frowning expression.

00:07:13.259 --> 00:07:15.449
Then she heard the sound it was making.

00:07:15.449 --> 00:07:19.710
Perhaps it was her years working with animals
at the shelter and her long-held sympathy

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:24.550
for them that had given her a sort of protective
instinct for all living things.

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:30.259
Or maybe it was just the bizarreness of the
situation, of having a rock with a mouth whimpering

00:07:30.259 --> 00:07:32.530
in pain on the floor in front of her.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:37.410
Either way, Harper felt compelled to pick
the lump of stone back up, and place it gently

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:38.580
on the desk.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:43.430
Its mouth seemed to smile a bit more as she
did; while it seemed creepy at first, the

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:47.940
compulsion to take care of this helpless little
rock only got stronger.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:51.930
Grabbing a pipette and pouring a glass of
water from the faucet, Harper started to squeeze

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.680
out droplets of water into the rock’s mouth.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:59.330
She had no idea how it was able to drink,
but that didn’t seem all that important

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:02.400
or relevant in order for her to care for it.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.990
After all, every time a parrot or duck or
crow was brought into the station, the procedure

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:08.250
was similar.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:12.490
Harper would have to pipette them water or
medicine, it didn’t matter how they drank

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:14.610
it just because they had beaks.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:19.379
It was the same with baby animals, puppies
and kittens especially, who might need bottle

00:08:19.379 --> 00:08:22.040
feeding if they were only a few weeks old.

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:26.330
As much as she was starting from scratch and
would normally be completely baffled as to

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:30.879
the best way she could care for a rock, right
now Harper was pulling on her experience at

00:08:30.879 --> 00:08:33.779
the shelter to nurse it back to health.

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:37.450
If she was, in fact, using her instincts and
not being…

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:39.630
influenced, somehow.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:44.219
Over the next few days, Harper looked after
her newfound patient as if it had been her

00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:45.649
lifelong pet.

00:08:45.649 --> 00:08:50.260
As to why she was taking such care of a rock
or even what she would eventually do with

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:53.130
it, Harper didn’t really have much of a
clue.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:57.470
But the answers to those questions didn’t
feel as important as looking after the rock.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:01.920
She’d leave for work in the morning, only
to count down the hours through her shift

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.770
until she got to go home and tend to the smiling
stone.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:09.700
Throughout the course of her working day,
she would pocket supplies to help her take

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:14.710
better care of her new ‘pet’; everything
from medicine in case it got sick or picked

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:20.060
up an infection of some kind, to bandages
and other materials she could use as bedding.

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:24.760
Grabbing a fresh, clean plastic container
for it instead of another cardboard box, Harper

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:28.149
was gradually fashioning a nest for the toothy
pebble.

00:09:28.149 --> 00:09:32.899
And for the most part, it seemed to be working,
some deep-seated feeling told her that her

00:09:32.899 --> 00:09:37.829
efforts to look after the rock was gradually
making it feel better every day…

00:09:37.829 --> 00:09:39.810
And then it started talking.

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:44.980
It wasn’t exactly articulate or eloquent,
it didn’t even seem to know that many words,

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:49.690
other than the two it was weakly repeating
when she came home from work one day.

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:56.430
“Need… eat…” it rasped, a hoarse whisper
passing through its unzipped lips.

00:09:56.430 --> 00:10:00.430
Rushing over to it, barely questioning it,
Harper tried filling up her pipette and giving

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:01.560
it more water.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:04.680
The rock spat it back out, before speaking
again.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:06.120
“No drink…

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:07.550
only eat…”

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:11.630
“What am I meant to feed you?”

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:16.020
she asked, once again not questioning the
absurdity of talking to a rock.

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:20.160
Maybe at this point, after getting over the
fact it had a mouth, it wasn’t much of a

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.770
shock that the stone also had a voice.

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:27.779
Or could her compelling need to care for it
made her oblivious to the strangeness of this

00:10:27.779 --> 00:10:28.779
situation?

00:10:28.779 --> 00:10:32.810
Of course, this wasn’t a situation that
many people had also found themselves in.

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:38.070
This meant that even a quick cursory internet
search for ‘what do pet rocks eat’ didn’t

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:42.940
yield many useful results, only a few joke
articles and nothing that actually gave Harper

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:45.130
an answer she could act on.

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:50.510
All the while, her rock was still croaking
out the same two words, repeating them on

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:55.130
a loop, its little voice getting more and
more forceful each time.

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:56.649
“Need… eat…

00:10:56.649 --> 00:10:58.170
Need eat!”

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:02.519
Low on options, Harper left some bread crusts
in the rock’s nest, hoping that would be

00:11:02.519 --> 00:11:05.649
enough to at least sate the rock’s obvious
hunger.

00:11:05.649 --> 00:11:09.900
It paused, either smelling or sensing that
it had been offered something to eat.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:16.140
“Feed…” it rasped, before opening its
mouth wide with a tiny, expectant ‘ahh’.

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:21.700
Picking up the crusts one by one, Harper broke
little pieces off and fed each bit to her

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:22.700
rock.

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:26.820
She sighed as a sense of overwhelming relief
came flooding over her, glad that she’d

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:29.730
found a way to feed the mouthy little mineral.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:32.240
If only it had worked for longer.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:37.600
Only a few hours later, Harper was met with
more scraping demands from her pet rock, the

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:40.370
insistent sound waking her up in the middle
of the night.

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:44.970
But this time, as she offered it more bread
scraps, it refused to eat them.

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:48.720
It was still the early hours of the morning
by the time that Harper had gotten through

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:52.810
offering the rock every different type of
food in her kitchen, eventually giving up

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:54.589
through sheer exhaustion.

00:11:54.589 --> 00:11:59.430
She folded her pillow over her ears, trying
to drown out the constant cries of:

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:01.050
“Need… eat…”

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:03.410
But it didn’t do enough to allow her to
get to sleep.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:08.470
At any rate, the feeling that she had to care
for this talking rock was keeping her awake

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:10.360
as much as the noise.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:14.899
Staring across the room, purposely facing
away from her pet rock, Harper’s eyes fell

00:12:14.899 --> 00:12:16.730
over her work uniform.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:21.820
The logo of the animal shelter stitched on
it was just about visible as the gentle glow

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:23.810
of street lamps bled through the curtains.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:28.000
And seeing it, along with her keys clipped
onto her belt that lay on the floor, gave

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.230
her an idea that might work.

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:36.280
Desperation and the need to sleep getting
the better of her, Harper climbed in her car,

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.930
having put a jacket and jeans over her pajamas.

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:43.980
The streets were all empty at this hour, almost
pitch dark and deathly silent, but it made

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:48.120
driving from her home to workplace a lot faster
than usual.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:52.860
All the while, the rock’s voice was getting
more and more demanding from its container

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:55.460
that she had strapped to the front passenger
seat.

00:12:55.460 --> 00:13:00.570
Sneaking up to the side door, Harper turned
her key in the lock, pulled it open, and slipped

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:01.600
indoors.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:05.410
The moment they heard the sound of the door
shutting, a number of the dogs that had been

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:10.620
resting securely in their cages woke up and
started barking, the noise loudly echoing

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:12.150
around the shelter.

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:16.339
Even though it drowned out the rock’s tiny
voice, it felt as if its words were still

00:13:16.339 --> 00:13:21.230
ringing in Harper’s ears, compelling her
to go through with something she really didn’t

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:22.230
want to.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:26.089
And yet, it felt like one of the only other
options she had left.

00:13:26.089 --> 00:13:31.200
She’d decided it would have to be the squirrel,
rationalizing the choice in her head.

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:36.610
In an unpleasant but purely calculated, logic-based
way, it made the most sense.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:41.009
Earlier that week, the bushy-tailed woodland
forager had been hit by a moped, it had barely

00:13:41.009 --> 00:13:46.079
survived given the number of its tiny bones
that had been broken in the force of the impact.

00:13:46.079 --> 00:13:51.209
Finding its cage, it was still asleep, heavily
sedated on the dosage of painkillers the shelter’s

00:13:51.209 --> 00:13:53.200
resident vet had given it.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:58.089
Gently scooping it out of the cage, feeling
its fur against her cupped hand, Harper looked

00:13:58.089 --> 00:14:01.170
at her pet rock, its container on the table.

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:03.329
She remembered that it liked to be fed directly.

00:14:03.329 --> 00:14:08.269
Swallowing an urge to be sick that was rising
at the back of her throat, Harper opened a

00:14:08.269 --> 00:14:12.529
drawer in the vet’s office and pulled out
a surgical scalpel.

00:14:12.529 --> 00:14:15.730
When her boss at the shelter heard there had
been a break-in and reviewed the footage on

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:19.360
the building’s security cameras, they were
shocked at what they saw.

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:23.579
Though the angle obscured what Harper was
doing, the blood leftover and one missing

00:14:23.579 --> 00:14:26.220
squirrel seemed to be enough to go on.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:30.149
That, coupled with the fact that she had broken
in after hours and the numerous supplies that

00:14:30.149 --> 00:14:34.690
had gone missing from the storage cupboard,
led to Harper being dismissed.

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:39.330
She was fired from her job at the animal shelter,
forced to hand over her keys and uniform.

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:43.650
While any other day this would have devastated
her, the events seemed to wash over Harper

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:44.980
pretty quickly.

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:50.260
She was more relieved – no, pleased with
herself – that the rock had stopped begging

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:51.690
her to be fed.

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:54.260
Her plan had worked… for now.

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:57.940
It took about a week for her pet rock to start
demanding food again.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:01.000
This time, just the sound of its voice made
Harper frantic.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:05.370
Any ordinary human food she offered wasn’t
enough, it didn’t want that.

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:10.560
She already knew exactly what it wanted, the
only thing she’d fed it that had sustained

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:11.560
it.

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:14.750
The big problem was that now she didn’t
have her keys, it would be near impossible

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:16.699
for her to get into the shelter again.

00:15:16.699 --> 00:15:23.410
Yet still, the forceful command to fed kept
up, day and night, hour after hour.

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:24.410
Until…

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:28.740
Harper considered the only thing she had left
to give her pet rock.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:33.199
Lifting it out of the warm nest she kept it
in, her fingers were gripping the smooth stone

00:15:33.199 --> 00:15:34.199
tightly.

00:15:34.199 --> 00:15:38.860
Her arm was tense, in fact, both were; one
as her muscles strained while she held the

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:41.100
rock, the other in anticipation.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:46.480
Hovering it above her opposite arm, Harper
could feel the tiny breaths of her pet against

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:52.700
her skin, almost like it was so excited to
eat again that it was panting with expectancy.

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:56.490
She put it back down on her desk, quickly
running into the bathroom.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:16:00.520
It wasn’t that she had changed her mind
– she couldn’t, all she knew was that

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:03.149
she had to feed her pet rock.

00:16:03.149 --> 00:16:07.630
Harper just needed to grab a cloth to put
in her mouth, clamping her jaws down on it

00:16:07.630 --> 00:16:13.699
to muffle her scream as she let the rock’s
teeth sink into her flesh, blood trickling

00:16:13.699 --> 00:16:15.230
down her arm.

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:19.460
Nobody saw much of Harper afterwards, her
friends from the shelter would continuously

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:22.660
call her cell phone, only to get no reply.

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:26.569
She barely paid any attention to the ringing,
eventually just letting her phone’s battery

00:16:26.569 --> 00:16:29.990
die and not even bothering to charge it back
up.

00:16:29.990 --> 00:16:34.949
It wasn’t important; to her, the only thing
that mattered was caring for her pet rock.

00:16:34.949 --> 00:16:39.510
In Harper’s head, it needed to be kept safe
– and fed – and it was her job to look

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:42.060
after it to the best of her ability.

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:46.649
She had enough leftover medical supplies that
she’d lifted from the shelter – bandages,

00:16:46.649 --> 00:16:51.600
antiseptic spray, rubbing alcohol, tranquilisers
– to treat her wounds.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:53.750
After a while, it almost stopped hurting.

00:16:53.750 --> 00:16:58.470
Knowing that she was helping keep her pet
rock fed, being so emotionally attached to

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:01.450
it made any of the pain more bearable.

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:05.200
Even when she woke up one morning to find
that her rock had rolled off her desk onto

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:07.590
her bed, it hardly phased her.

00:17:07.590 --> 00:17:12.070
Nor did seeing it had gradually eaten her
leg during the night, gnawing through tendons

00:17:12.070 --> 00:17:16.960
and bone as she slept, leaving her sheets
still slick with blood when she awoke.

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:21.080
Despite having lost most of her capacity for
rational thought, Harper decided it would

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:23.920
be best to plan ahead for when she was gone.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:29.440
She knew, through some twisted form of logic,
that she wasn’t going to last forever.

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:34.660
There was only so much of her; she’d only
be able to feed her pet rock for so long.

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:39.330
Harper held a pen in her hand as best she
could with all the fingers she was now missing.

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:44.950
She scrawled out a note to whoever found what
was left of her, in messy, uncoordinated handwriting.

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:47.730
‘Please care for my pet rock.’

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:52.630
If keeping an SCP as a pet is an idea that
appeals to you, but you want one that doesn’t

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:58.110
come with the added danger of eating you alive,
then why not go and check out “SCP-529 JOSIE

00:17:58.110 --> 00:17:59.110
THE HALF CAT”.

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:03.820
Or, if you aren’t really a cat person – or
a half-cat person – but still want to adopt

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:08.880
your own anomaly, then we might just have
the perfect list of candidates in “TOP FOURTEEN

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:10.670
FRIENDLIEST SCPS”.

